  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest surface analysis shows a weak surface \nlow over the SC Pee Dee region, gradually lifting along a \nstalled frontal boundary draped across the coastal plains of the\nCarolinas. Aloft, stout mid-level trough remains in place with \na particularly strong shortwave pivoting around its base this \nafternoon.\n\nAfter some surprisingly strong convection this morning, eastern\nNC has cleared out nicely with ample sunshine allowing \ntemperatures to rebound back into the mid to upper 80s with dew \npoints, especially along the coast, in the mid to upper 70s. 18z\nMHX RAOB measured MLCAPE near 3000 J/kg and a whopping 6500 J/kg\nsurface-based, plus effective shear of 45 kt. All told, this \npoints to a more favorable pre- storm environment than \nyesterday, favoring a combination of multicell clusters and \nsupercells (local RAOB measured a supercell composite of 18.7\nand an STP of 2.1). Damaging winds are the primary threat for \nall modes with considerable dry air aloft inferring DCAPEs of \n1200+ J/kg. Supercells will also pose a hail risk. Supercell \ninteractions with sea/sound breezes and remnant outflow from \nmorning convection could also spin up a tornado. \n\nSPC has maintained most of the Outer Banks and coastal locales \ndown to the SC border in a Slight (Level 2/5) risk, with a \nmarginal risk (Level 1/5) farther inland. Given this morning's \nconvection and resulting stabilization, the highest risk has now\nshifted to the south and west, focused from roughly New Bern SW\ntowards the NC/SC state line. Convection is most likely from \n17z through 23z today, with the bulk of activity dissipating \nafter sunset. Any remnant activity should fully dissipate after \nmidnight when the last shortwave pushes off the coast and SFC \nlow departing to the NE, leading to a less conducive flow \npattern through the column.\n\nPWATs are down slightly but given the prolonged period of\nunsettled weather torrential rainfall could once again pose a\nflash flooding risk, especially in urban and poor drainage\nareas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continues to suggest tomorrow will be \nprimarily dry with only an isolated shower or storm possible on \narea breezes in the afternoon. On Wednesday, surface flow \nbecomes more onshore kicking off a period of increasing moisture\nand instability across the Southeast U.S. coastline with a \nmodest uptick of shower and thunderstorm coverage. Another wave \nof low pressure is forecast to lift along the offshore frontal \nboundary Thursday into Friday, pulling it back towards the coast\nand providing a better focal point for more widespread coverage\nof showers and storms into the weekend.",
